Day 9: Building and Running a Custom Docker Image

Overview

Today I practiced creating a custom Docker image using a Dockerfile, packaging my own HTML website into an Nginx image, and running it as a container.

1. Project Structure

Created a new project with the following structure:

day9-docker-project/
├── Dockerfile
└── website/
    └── index.html

2. Created a Custom Dockerfile

FROM nginx:latest
COPY website/ /usr/share/nginx/html/

Instructions Learned

- "FROM" — selects the base image.
- "COPY" — copies files from the build context into the image.
- Nginx default website directory — "/usr/share/nginx/html/".

3. Built a Custom Docker Image

sudo docker build -t day9-website:v1 .

Learned how to build a custom image from a Dockerfile and assign a name and tag to it.

4. Verified the Image

sudo docker images

Confirmed that "day9-website:v1" was successfully created.

5. Ran a Container

sudo docker run -d --name day9-website-container -p 8085:80 day9-website:v1

Learned how to run a custom image as a container and map host port 8085 to container port 80.

6. Website Verification

- Opened the website using "http://localhost:8085".
- Confirmed that the custom website was accessible in the browser.
- Verified that "index.html" was present inside the container.

7. Container Inspection

sudo docker exec day9-website-container ls /usr/share/nginx/html/

sudo docker exec day9-website-container cat /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html

Learned how to execute commands inside a running container and inspect files without stopping it.

Key Learnings

- Creating a custom image using a Dockerfile.
- Using Nginx as a base image.
- Copying website files into the container's web directory.
- Building and tagging Docker images.
- Running containers with port mapping.
- Verifying website files and content inside a container using "docker exec".

  Day 9 Completed.
